            "count": 0,
            "text": "## Summary\n\nComparison, bitwise, and integer or mixed-type arithmetic operators run 1.3 to 5 times as fast on vectors when no operand is recycled. Enhancement: a performance improvement, results do not change.\n\n## Details\n\n`MOD_ITERATE1` and `MOD_ITERATE2` in `R_ext/Itermacros.h` test every operand index for wrapping around on every iteration. With the patch they compare the lengths once and, when each operand is as long as the result or has length one, run a loop without those tests, which compilers can then vectorize. No call site changes; the loop body sees the same index values.\n\nBenchmark: attached as `mod-iterate-bench.R`. For vectors of 10,000 elements, `x < y` goes from 0.83 to 0.26 ns per element with Apple clang and from 0.90 to 0.34 with GCC 13.3 (both arm64). Double arithmetic and recycled operands are unchanged.\n\nProposed patch: attached as `mod-iterate-no-recycling.diff`, against r90971. The loop body is now expanded once per loop, and the header is installed, so packages using these macros get the same loops. `make check` passes, and all callers give identical results with and without the patch.\n\nThe patch is also submitted as https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/529, where CI results and more timings can be seen.\n\n## AI Disclosure\n\nThis patch was prepared with the assistance of Claude Code. The issue was discovered while looking for loops in R that compilers cannot vectorize, as a follow-up to bug 19364.",
